        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Great Wall of China</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 700 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">The states of the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">First Emperor of Qin</a>, who joined the northern walls; the Han, the Northern dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, who repaired and extended them; the Northern Wei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, Jin and Western Xia, who built walls further north; and above all the Ming</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">Walls, ramparts, trenches, watchtowers, passes and fortresses of rammed earth, stone and wood and, under the Ming, of brick bound with sticky <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> mortar; the sites mapped in 2012 total 21,196.18 km of walls and trenches, the Ming wall 8,850 km</span></div>
