    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Great Wall is many walls, not one: the fortifications of rival states from the 7th century BCE, joined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> and rebuilt by later dynasties, mapped in 2012 at 21,196.18 km of walls and trenches.</li><li>The brick and stone walls best known today are Ming work, built after the Ming defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> at the Battle of Tumu, with up to 25,000 watchtowers.</li><li>Under the Qing, whose rule extended beyond it, work on the wall for border defence stopped; a 2012 report found that 22% of the Ming wall has disappeared.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sticky <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> mortar, made of sticky rice soup mixed with slaked lime, was widely used to hold the wall’s bricks together; contrary to urban legend, no human bones or body parts were ever built into it.</p></aside>
    </section>

